WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.700
- If I loved you, not the woman

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.135
that I'm trying to make you be--

00:00:05.135 --> 00:00:07.710
- What is happening right now?

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.690
I think it's a beautiful day
to save lives, don't you agree?

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:11.523
(squeals)

00:00:11.523 --> 00:00:12.620
- I'm gonna like being married.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.286
It's the wedding part that's ridiculous.

00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:16.571
(laughing)

00:00:16.571 --> 00:00:17.447
(pager beeping)

00:00:17.447 --> 00:00:18.280
(groans)

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:19.113
Party's over.

00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:19.946
- You know what's funny?

00:00:19.946 --> 00:00:21.610
Even though this show was
filmed so long ago, this scene

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.330
of them hanging around and
having pagers still rings true.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.622
All my residents still
carry their beepers around

00:00:27.622 --> 00:00:29.820
and they get paged, and
they have to interrupt what

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.770
they're doing to go
respond to an emergency.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.140
Now, we're not surgical residents,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.470
we're family medicine residents,

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.950
but equally as important,
just on different terms.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:38.640
- You marrying Burke, it's a sign.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.810
A sign that people like
you and me can do this.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.790
Be healthy, be happy.

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.430
- Did she just steal my line in the show?

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.180
- They found the fourth climber.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.273
Vitals are stabilizing after fluids, but--

00:00:50.273 --> 00:00:51.106
- I thought he was dead.

00:00:51.106 --> 00:00:51.990
- He was, according to his friends.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.720
Did the CT show him bleeding?

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.799
- We haven't done a CT yet.

00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:55.632
- I don't understand.

00:00:55.632 --> 00:00:56.465
You said there was a head trauma.

00:00:56.465 --> 00:00:58.600
- Yeah, there's been a little head trauma.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.780
Ice axe to the head.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.910
- Whoa.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.420
So you really need, probably,
a neurosurgery consult

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:07.170
at this point for having an ice pick stuck

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:08.210
in this gentleman's head.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.460
Now, as I told you
earlier on this channel,

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.470
if you ever get hit with something

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.520
and it stays inserted into you,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.360
a knife, an ice pick, whatever it is,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.030
you don't take it out because
it's putting pressure on there

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:21.380
to prevent you from bleeding out.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.780
So, if you take that ice pick
out, what's gonna happen?

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.720
The person's gonna bleed,
especially from the skull.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.087
If you ever needed to get
staples placed in your scalp

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:32.480
because you cut yourself,
you know how much it bleeds.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.330
Even the smallest little cut on the scalp

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.420
bleeds a tremendous amount.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.740
It's actually really scary.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.180
But it's only because there's a lot

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.660
of blood supply to that area, not because

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.520
there's a lot of damage to that area.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.720
So just keep that in mind.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.270
- My wife came into my hospital

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.287
and you neglected to tell me?

00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:48.130
- Doctor-patient confidentiality.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.228
- It's actually a very
difficult place to find yourself

00:01:50.228 --> 00:01:54.250
because, if you have one of
your colleagues, family members

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.460
as your patient, you want
to share and you feel

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.130
like you should be able to
share, but in reality, you can't.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:04.860
I actually treat a lot of my
colleagues, even my attendings,

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.810
when I was a resident, their children.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.960
And as a result, the things
that their children tell me,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.080
I can't share with them.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.440
Only when they're brought
into the room by the patient

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.290
or the patient expressed the fact

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:16.950
that I can discuss their case with them,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.189
do I then incorporate them
into the conversation.

00:02:20.189 --> 00:02:22.870
But in reality, it's two separate people.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.020
Just because I know
both of you doesn't mean

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.120
I can freely talk about
your care to one another.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.540
There's some things we
share with our family,

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.490
some things we don't, and that's a right.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:31.610
- She's unconscious.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.612
There are medical decisions to be made!

00:02:33.612 --> 00:02:35.730
Legally, I have the right to know!

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:36.600
I have a right!

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.993
- She might be having a miscarriage.

00:02:41.412 --> 00:02:43.501
- What's wrong with me?

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:45.214
- I'm going to explain
everything to you, I promise.

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:48.370
But right now, we need to get
you up to the surgical floor.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:49.370
- [Patient] Surgery?

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.020
What about my babies?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.870
- Well, I think those
babies are coming out today.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.443
- But, I haven't decided.

00:02:58.877 --> 00:03:00.260
I haven't chosen parents.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.770
I need time to decide.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.470
- It looked like he was doing
an ultrasound of her heart,

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.790
which is an echocardiogram.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.740
I didn't follow the case
from the previous story,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.290
but it sounds like she's pregnant

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.040
and she's acting as a
surrogate to give the child

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.560
to one of the two families there.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.370
I wonder what could be
going on with her heart

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.680
that they need to take
her for an operation?

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.220
The medical curiosity in me is a-peaking!

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.053
- How's Rena?

00:03:21.053 --> 00:03:22.504
- I need to get a CT adrenal,

00:03:22.504 --> 00:03:24.603
but I'm pretty sure it's
a tear in her aorta.

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:25.630
Her heart is about to blow.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:26.820
- Whoa!

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.340
So, dissecting an aortic
aneurysm is when there's

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:33.168
an aneurysm within the
aorta, which is the largest

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:35.180
blood vessel that comes off of the heart

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.920
and brings blood to the rest of your body.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.360
An aneurysm, first of all,
is a weakness in the wall

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.450
where it actually protrudes out.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.690
So, if you imagine your
artery being like a balloon,

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.660
and then one area of the
balloon is very weak,

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:48.930
so it creates almost like an outpouching.

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.850
When that happens, that wall's
very susceptible to bursting

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:54.670
and rupturing because it
doesn't have all the layers

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:57.570
that the rest of the artery has.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.720
So, what can happen is
you can have a dissecting

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.980
aortic aneurysm, where it's getting so bad

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.090
that that weak arterial wall
is literally about to burst,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.780
and that patient can
rightfully bleed out and die

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.460
because of all the bleeding internally.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.070
So you have to go for surgery immediately.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.631
I just don't know how this
is happening in such a calm

00:04:15.631 --> 00:04:18.880
situation because it's
really a medical emergency

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.190
or a surgical emergency where you need

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.270
to go, go, go, and start the operation.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:23.150
- I didn't page you.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.790
Go home and get some sleep.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:25.754
- Yeah, but I'm here already, so--

00:04:25.754 --> 00:04:29.207
- You're getting married
in less than 12 hours.

00:04:29.207 --> 00:04:30.900
- (music drowns out speaker)

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.220
- Well, Burke's getting married
too, and he gets to operate.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.690
- There's only one Preston Burke.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.340
- There's only one Christina Yang.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:39.920
- I've given you the
day off, the whole day.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.030
It's a kindness, take it.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:41.863
It's your wedding day.

00:04:41.863 --> 00:04:43.100
There will always be more surgeries.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:43.933
- But--

00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:45.390
- Really, what would you rather be doing?

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.210
Prepping a patient or
prepping for your wedding?

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.043
Go home.

00:04:48.043 --> 00:04:50.190
- Those who love our jobs
and who are passionate

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.420
about our jobs, we want to be there.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.130
But, at times, we have to pump the brakes

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.770
and realize that, if we want
to perform better at work,

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.950
it's actually ideal to take some time off.

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:03.410
This is why one of the biggest
problems that people face

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.860
is that as they make more
money, you would think

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.630
they would become more
financially independent,

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:09.500
therefore take more time
off, but in reality,

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.770
they just become slaves to
their money and to their job.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.938
It's really important to not burn out

00:05:13.938 --> 00:05:16.640
because too many people in
our society right now are

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.470
working day in and day
out, and there's times

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.140
we need to do that and we
need to put our foot down,

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.120
but then there's also times
where we need to understand that

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.980
a break will actually
yield better results.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:27.941
- They recovered Lonnie's body?

00:05:27.941 --> 00:05:29.863
- Not his body, he's alive.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:33.050
So why don't you tell
me what really happened

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:34.020
up there with the mountain?

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.090
- So, I'm assuming,
because I don't know a lot

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.160
about this case, they're on a mountain,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.440
they were trapped, there's
frostbite happening.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.090
Frostbite generally
happens on areas that have

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.650
less circulation, and
areas that not only have

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.370
less circulation, but
are ones that your body

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.890
will decrease blood flow to
in order to conserve warmth.

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.990
So, for example, if you
ever see your hands get blue

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.220
when you're really cold, that's your body

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.870
naturally protecting
yourself from losing heat.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:04.490
What it does is redirects
the blood back to your core

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:07.520
so that you stay warm, your
vital organs stay well profuse

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.030
with blood flow, and you
don't need a lot of blood flow

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:11.970
to keep your hands alive.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.610
So, if they look blue,
that's what's happening.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.910
There's also a condition
called Raynaud's Phenomenon.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:18.950
You have an ultrasensitive
reaction to cold.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.120
So, just being in a cold location
for a short period of time

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:23.320
will make your hands turn blue.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.500
A simple test for that is to
put your hand in cold water,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.470
and I'm not recommending you do this,

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.340
but you could put your hand in cold water

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.240
and take it right out.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.453
If your hand turns blue really quickly,

00:06:30.453 --> 00:06:32.713
you have what's known as Raynaud's.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.100
- It was an accident.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.450
- I feel like it's not McDreamy's place

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.030
to make the decision on whether
or not it was an accident.

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.840
In this scenario, if I have
people talking about self-harm,

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.220
or I don't know what you would call this,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.420
like an assault-type situation.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.040
Even if you think it may be
on accident or self-defense,

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:56.580
you have to get the authorities involved.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.170
I think that's the ideal situation.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.150
Maybe not right in this
moment because no one's

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:02.157
escaping here, they all
look really injured,

00:07:02.157 --> 00:07:05.270
but at some point, you have to
get the authorities involved.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:06.620
- So, should I be worried or something?

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.640
- Should you be worried
that I met a woman?

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:09.800
No.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.900
Should you worried that, for me,

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.153
flirting with that woman was
the highlight of my week?

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.073
Yeah, you should be worried or something.

00:07:17.981 --> 00:07:20.660
- (laughs) What kind of threat was that?

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.470
That's manipulative, McDreamy.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:24.303
Don't do that.

00:07:24.303 --> 00:07:26.410
- I just checked, Chief
Resident hasn't been posted yet.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:27.290
- No, no, I wasn't looking for--

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:28.662
- Yeah, you were.

00:07:28.662 --> 00:07:30.280
- (laughs) Yeah okay, I was.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.820
- For those of you who
don't know, Chief Resident

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.320
is a really important position.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.610
Even more important when
you're a surgical resident.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:36.443
I'll give you an example.

00:07:36.443 --> 00:07:39.810
In family medicine, the senior
attendings all sit together

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.020
on a committee and nominate

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.680
who's going to be the Chief Resident.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.172
Generally, the way it works
is the Chief Resident,

00:07:45.172 --> 00:07:48.120
in their last year of residency,
takes on the additional

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.540
role of doing all the
scheduling, all the organizing

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.390
of the rest of the junior residents.

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.270
In internal medicine,
they may actually stay on

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:58.420
for a fourth additional year.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:02.070
To do that, consult on difficult
cases, work in specialties

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.210
that they feel they need more help in,

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.430
or they use it as an opportunity
to showcase themselves

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:09.970
for fellowship positions
that they may have not gotten

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:12.130
or to make themselves more competitive

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:13.850
in the upcoming match.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:17.910
Very prestigious, but very
difficult to be successful in.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.430
- Housing a human for nine long months

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.223
is all that I can think about.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:25.810
The thoughts are invading
the surgeries that I love,

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:30.810
and it's hormonal and horrible,
but it is happening to me.

00:08:36.177 --> 00:08:37.772
- You want to have a baby.

00:08:37.772 --> 00:08:40.930
- (laughs) Did one resident
just ask another resident

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.690
to impregnate her
because she wants a baby?

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.810
I hope I'm missing something
and they're actually

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.750
in a long-term relationship,
and that was their way

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:48.670
of talking about it.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:49.870
- Pressure's dropped to 60.

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:50.740
She's bringing it down.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:51.573
Edison, how much longer?

00:08:51.573 --> 00:08:52.640
- Rupturing the membrane now.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.470
I just need two minutes
to get these babies out.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.303
- Make it one.

00:08:55.303 --> 00:08:56.136
She's flat-lined.

00:08:56.136 --> 00:08:57.240
Stephen, start compressions.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.130
- Yay, they're starting chest compressions

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.030
before shocking the patient first!

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.530
I'm sorry I'm so excited
that a patient's flat-lining,

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:06.510
but I'm just happy that he
said start chest compressions

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.390
before whipping out the machine to shock.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:09.559
- Clamp.

00:09:09.559 --> 00:09:10.494
- Come on, come on.

00:09:10.494 --> 00:09:11.327
(cries)

00:09:11.327 --> 00:09:12.160
- Second baby's out.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:12.993
Okay, go.

00:09:12.993 --> 00:09:13.826
- Oh, my God.

00:09:13.826 --> 00:09:15.940
But look at those horrible
chest compressions

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:17.430
happening in the background.

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:18.263
Oh, my God.

00:09:18.263 --> 00:09:22.070
The doctors, they're
going like this. (laughs)

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:22.903
Hold on.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:25.920
That is not 100 beats per minutes.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.210
That's not two inches deep.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.270
Chest compressions, chest
compressions, chest compressions!

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.740
I think I'm yelling it every video.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:31.990
- Is he going to wake up?

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:34.202
- Do you want him to?

00:09:34.202 --> 00:09:35.260
- What?

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.680
- The axe in his head was
put there deliberately.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.530
I just want to give you three
guys another opportunity

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.200
to tell me the truth.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.128
- You're not a cop!

00:09:43.128 --> 00:09:44.160
(groans)

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:44.993
- What's going on?

00:09:44.993 --> 00:09:47.965
- She started cramping and then
she got really diaphoretic.

00:09:47.965 --> 00:09:49.370
- Alert (background noise drowns
out speaker) to a possible

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:51.720
DNC due to an incomplete miscarriage.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.750
- So, a DNC is a dilation and curettage.

00:09:54.750 --> 00:09:56.340
It's basically a medical procedure

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.500
to get the uterine contents out.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:02.760
That can be really important
if there is bleeding going on,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.030
you're trying to figure out

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.710
where the source of
bleeding is coming from.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:07.640
This is something you
need to do very urgently.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.268
I understand why, because
this patient is bleeding out,

00:10:10.268 --> 00:10:11.830
diaphoretic, cramping ...

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.620
Cramping means that
there's bleeding, probably,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.200
happening in the uterine cavity.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:16.420
We need to diagnosis and treat

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.040
it simultaneously with a DNC.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:20.180
- She went into early DIC.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.603
I gave her blood, platelets, FFP.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.950
Pregnancy at 52 is dangerous.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:27.190
I tried everything.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.707
I couldn't save ...

00:10:28.707 --> 00:10:31.540
(emotional music)

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:35.823
- Oh, wow.

00:10:37.379 --> 00:10:38.212
(exhales)

00:10:38.212 --> 00:10:39.513
Oh, the babies.

00:10:39.513 --> 00:10:41.000
(groans)

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:41.843
- I thought ...

00:10:43.266 --> 00:10:44.533
I thought ...

00:10:45.465 --> 00:10:47.530
For a second, I thought I had lost you.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:50.850
- I thought he lost her
too, because DIC can be a

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.040
very deadly, and quickly deadly condition.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.830
It stands for disseminated
intravascular coagulation.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.850
It's basically when inside your blood,

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:03.430
you use up all the clotting
factors and you start clotting,

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:04.910
so two things happen.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:07.760
The more platelets you have,
the more clotting that happens,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.520
but then you also use up all
your platelets so that you

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:11.870
could bleed from other areas.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:12.970
Very unique situation.

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:15.190
There's very specific
protocols we have to follow

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.540
in order to make sure these
patients A) don't bleed out,

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:20.290
and B) don't form clots
in areas where they can be

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:22.760
life-threatening: brain,
heart, lungs, etc.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.300
I think that might have
been the best explanation

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:27.030
of DIC I've ever given
in my life. (laughs)

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.253
- He's a much better man than I thought.

00:11:30.759 --> 00:11:34.777
Looking over his initial
judgments, and he'll sit with you,

00:11:36.117 --> 00:11:37.637
- Is it him?

00:11:37.637 --> 00:11:40.813
And he'll make you drink your water.

00:11:40.813 --> 00:11:43.700
(dramatic music)

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.093
- He's the papa bear!

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.429
I mean, sad that he lost his child, God.

00:11:48.429 --> 00:11:50.550
All right, I got to really
adjust my emotions here.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:53.733
- Because I'm your best
friend, and because I love you.

00:11:57.926 --> 00:12:02.370
I also have to say that
I'm in love with you.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.840
- I officially want to
check what they're pumping

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.710
into the air conditioning
units in Seattle Grace's

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:13.500
whatever-the-name-is hospital is.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.610
I don't know if they're
pumping pheromones in there,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:17.270
angel, Cupid arrows ...

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:19.060
Everyone's in love with everybody.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.190
Everyone wants to cheat on everybody.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.400
People want to get married,
they don't want to get married.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.530
They want to have babies, what?

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:25.820
This is the most dysfunctional

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.630
group of humans I've ever seen.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:29.662
- I want you to give me a reason to stay.

00:12:29.662 --> 00:12:33.240
- Even the patients are falling
in love with the doctors!

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.170
I can't even, I lost count of how many

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:38.840
love triangles and circles and squares.

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:41.050
I feel like, in order to
understand their relationships

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.112
fully, I got to bring out the

00:12:42.112 --> 00:12:43.990
Pythagorean theorem on this one.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:47.190
I mean, for God's sakes, Grey's
Anatomy, keep it together.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:48.300
- You don't want me?

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.360
- Maybe I do.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:50.283
- No, you don't.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:53.950
You want Ava.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:55.150
- She's not Ava anymore, she--

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:57.030
- (laughs) I know why this is

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.100
one of the top fan-favorite episodes.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.310
Because everyone's in love with every--

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:02.920
Is it something about weddings?

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:04.750
Or is this every Grey's Anatomy episode?

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:06.770
For some reason, every
episode I watched before this,

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.860
there was a lot of medical
stuff, a little bit of romance.

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:13.640
This is everyone is on top
of everybody all the time.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:16.660
- I'm up there waiting for
you to come down the aisle,

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:18.880
and I know you don't want to come.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:20.930
I know you don't want to
come, but that you'll come

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:22.280
anyway because you love me.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:30.120
And if I loved you, if I
loved you, not the woman that

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:32.350
I'm trying to make you be,
not the woman that I hope--

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:35.010
- What is happening right now?

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:36.670
Did they need to make everything

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.470
more dramatic than it needs to be?

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:40.830
You decide to get married,
you love each other,

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.080
but the person's hesitant, so now

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.210
you're being worried
that they're hesitant,

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.580
and your love contradicts their love.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.020
We should just change the
name of this episode to

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.400
Dr. Mike Loses His Mind
Over Grey's Anatomy

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:53.490
instead of react.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:54.323
Just no reacting.

00:13:54.323 --> 00:13:55.980
I'm just losing my mind at this point.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.820
I feel like you guys missed out my latest

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.930
medical memes review and my hospital vlog,

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.860
so if you did, click
one of those bad boys.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.350
And, as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:07.767
(calm music)

